# Merge behaviour for files two machines both write to.
#
# This repo is written from two places: a local session and a cloud routine that
# starts from a fresh clone. Both append to the same logs on the same day, so
# every sync is a merge — and a plain merge turns two new entries into a
# conflict over a line neither of them changed.
#
# Two kinds of file, two rules.
#
# 1. Append-only logs: take both sides. A log's entries do not contend with each
#    other, so union is the correct result rather than a convenience.
ops/daily-log.md           merge=union
ops/feedback-log.md        merge=union

# 2. Snapshot files describe the current state, so they have ONE writer at a
#    time and are replaced rather than appended to. They are deliberately left
#    on the default merge: a snapshot merged from two machines describes neither
#    of them, and a visible conflict is the right outcome. Named here so the
#    omission reads as a decision rather than an oversight:
#      ops/priorities.md · ops/pipeline.md · ops/customers.md
#      ops/roadmap-signals.md · ops/direction-register.md
#      ops/chokepoint-register.md
#
# `merge=union` needs no driver — git has it built in.
